From 6eeaff78eb53ef55206fef2d0a3f64ff53dd59f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nussaccino <1345551+Nussaccino@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 20:13:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] DLSS Super Resolution and DLAA through NVIDIA Streamline
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Settings → Graphics → Anti-aliasing → DLSS / DLAA, with its quality (DLAA,
Quality, Balanced, Performance, Ultra performance) in the render scale's row,
and the same in the game menu's Options → Graphics, where another quality is
taken at once; `dlss=` in settings.cfg, OMSI_DLSS to override. Windows,
DirectX 12 (taken when DLSS is on) and a GeForce RTX: Streamline's DLLs are
loaded at run time from beside the game (or OMSI_STREAMLINE_DIR), nothing of
NVIDIA's is linked or shipped. Without them, or on another card or graphics
API, the picture is drawn as before, with the MSAA the settings hold.

- dlss.rs: Streamline 2.14's C layout in Rust (sizes checked at compile
  time); DLSS recorded into a D3D12 command list of ours on wgpu's queue
  between the scene's submit and the HUD's; a swap chain that presents
  nothing, so that Streamline's end-of-frame bookkeeping runs with manual
  hooking
- the projection jittered (Halton 2,3); the depth prepass writes motion
  vectors - the camera's and every object's, from last frame's model
  matrices - with the same depth as the main pass (vs_main's vertex shared).
  The motion passes are in dlss_motion.wgsl, in the scene module only where
  its arrays are storage buffers (never on the chips reading them from
  textures)
- the rain films' drops get their pane's motion and DLSS's BiasCurrentColor
  mask: they neither streak nor show twice once DLSS scales up
- DLSS's result decoded from sRGB on its way into an sRGB window
